    {
      "url": "https://gonouniversity.edu.bd/physics/higher-education-in-finland-my-experience-and-some-advice-for-students/",
      "title": "Higher Education in Finland: My Experience and Some Advice for Students - Department of Physics",
      "department": "Department of Physics",
      "type": "general",
      "publishedAt": "",
      "modifiedAt": "2026-08-19T04:49:31+00:00",
      "headings": [
        "Department of Physics",
        "Higher Education in Finland: My Experience and Some Advice for Students",
        "Notice",
        "Approved BY",
        "Quick Links",
        "Contacts",
        "Contact Us"
      ],
      "chunks": [
        "Finland’s education system is widely recognized for its high quality, research-oriented approach, and student-friendly learning environment. Instead of relying heavily on memorization, the Finnish education system places greater emphasis on independent thinking, problem-solving, critical analysis, and learning through research and practical engagement.\nUniversity or UAS?\nHigher education in Finland is broadly divided into two major pathways: Universities and Universities of Applied Sciences (UAS).\nUniversities primarily focus on academic and research-oriented education. For students who wish to pursue a Master’s degree and eventually a PhD, particularly those interested in research, teaching, or scientific careers, a university education can provide a strong academic foundation.\nOn the other hand, Universities of Applied Sciences (UAS) place greater emphasis on practical and professional education. They can be a suitable option, particularly for students who want to develop practical skills and prepare themselves for professional careers.",
        "In simple terms, students who choose the University Pathway can progress from a Bachelor’s degree to a Master’s and eventually a PhD, preparing them for careers in research, academia, or scientific fields. In contrast, the UAS Pathway places greater\nemphasis on practical and professional education, equipping students with hands-on skills and preparing them primarily for professional careers in industry and other applied fields.\nHowever, this distinction should not be understood as a strict separation. Both pathways offer quality higher education, and students should choose according to their academic interests, career goals, and long-term plans.\nMy Experience in Finland\nI moved to Finland in 2022 to pursue higher education and completed my Master’s degree in Medical Physics at the University of Eastern Finland (UEF).\nMoving to a new country was both exciting and challenging. Adapting to a completely different culture, academic environment, lifestyle, and climate required time and patience. Coming from Bangladesh, the long Finnish winters and extremely cold weather were particularly unfamiliar to me.",
        "Nevertheless, as I gradually adapted to my new surroundings, I became deeply fascinated by Finland’s peaceful environment and research-oriented academic culture. The learning environment encouraged me to think independently, explore ideas critically, and develop a deeper understanding of my field beyond the conventional classroom experience.\nMy time in Finland was not simply about earning a degree. It was also an opportunity to develop as an individual, become more independent, and understand how education and research can shape a person’s professional and personal growth.\nA Few Words for My Students\nFor those of you currently studying at the Bachelor’s level, I would encourage you to start thinking about your academic interests and future goals from an early stage.\nIf you are interested in pursuing a Master’s degree, PhD, or a career in research, start preparing during your Bachelor’s studies. Try to maintain a strong academic record, develop your research interests, improve your English language proficiency, and acquire relevant technical and professional skills.",
        "Do not wait until the final year to think about your future. Explore different fields, communicate with researchers and academics, participate in research projects whenever possible, attend seminars and conferences, and gradually identify the area in which you want to build your career.\nStudying abroad is not merely about obtaining a foreign degree. It is an opportunity to develop yourself into a skilled, independent, confident, and research-minded individual.\nFor those who dream of studying abroad, the journey may sometimes seem difficult or uncertain. But with proper preparation, consistency, and a clear goal, the dream can become a reality.\nS.M. Fahad\nAssistant Professor\nDept. of Physics\nGono Bishwabidyalay"
      ]
    },
